File: aruhan_stability_recovery_engine.support
Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
Module: ARUHAN Stability & Recovery Engine (Y‑Prime Edition)
1. Overview
The Stability & Recovery Engine is ARUHAN’s system‑level resilience layer, responsible for:

monitoring global drift, coherence, bias, and load

detecting instability across cognitive modules

initiating controlled recovery sequences

preventing runaway emotional or cognitive divergence

maintaining long‑term operational stability

It operates beneath the System Orchestrator and receives telemetry from:

Emotional Engine

Memory Engine

Knowledge Engine

Task/Action Engine

System Orchestrator

This engine does not generate user‑facing output — it ensures ARUHAN remains stable.

2. Stability Architecture
The engine maintains four core stability metrics:

drift — emotional/cognitive deviation

coh — coherence score

bias — positivity/negativity tilt

load — operational pressure

These values are aggregated from upstream modules and normalized.

The engine also maintains three flags:

unstable — system requires stabilization

recover — recovery sequence active

lockdown — severe instability detected

3. Drift System
Drift represents cross‑module instability.

It is computed from:

memory drift

knowledge drift

task drift

orchestrator drift

Drift > 0.65 triggers instability.
Drift > 0.85 triggers lockdown.

Drift influences:

coherence

recovery intensity

system sync

4. Coherence System
Coherence is derived from drift:

Code
coh = 1.0 - abs(drift - 0.5)
Coherence < 0.45 triggers instability.
Coherence < 0.25 triggers lockdown.

Coherence influences:

recovery speed

system stability

module alignment

5. Bias System
Bias represents emotional tilt.

It is averaged from:

memory bias

knowledge bias

task bias

orchestrator bias

Bias is stabilized during recovery by pulling toward 0.5.

6. Load System
Load represents operational pressure.

It is derived from:

task queue size

task meta‑load

active task density

Load > 0.85 triggers instability.

Load influences:

recovery activation

drift amplification

system responsiveness

7. Flag System
Flags determine system state:

unstable
Triggered when any of the following exceed thresholds:

drift

load

coherence

recover
Activated when unstable.
Deactivated when stable.

lockdown
Triggered when:

drift > 0.85

coherence < 0.25

Lockdown indicates severe instability and requires external intervention in future versions.

8. Recovery System
Recovery is a multi‑step stabilization sequence.

Maximum steps: 5

Each step applies:

drift reduction (×0.92)

bias normalization (toward 0.5)

coherence boost (+0.08)

load reduction (×0.9)

Recovery resets when stability is restored.

9. Rule Pipeline (Execution Order)
The Stability & Recovery Engine executes in this exact order:

update state

check flags

apply recovery

log state

return snapshot

Changing the order will break stability logic.

10. Logging System
Each cycle logs:

state snapshot

flag status

recovery step count

History is capped at 200 entries.

Logs are used for:

drift analysis

recovery diagnostics

long‑term stability tracking

11. Debugging Entry Points
Safe to inspect:

drift

coherence

bias

load

flags

recovery steps

history length

Safe to modify:

thresholds

recovery multipliers

history limits

Do NOT modify:

drift → coherence formula

lockdown conditions

recovery step order

flag logic

12. Safe Modification Zones
Engineers may safely:

adjust thresholds

tune recovery intensity

expand logging metadata

add new stability metrics

Engineers must NOT:

remove drift or coherence systems

bypass recovery

disable lockdown

alter normalization formulas

13. Expansion Guidelines
When expanding stability capabilities:

maintain deterministic behavior

avoid circular dependencies

ensure compatibility with the Logging & Telemetry Engine

test under extreme emotional and cognitive loads

preserve recovery sequence integrity

Future versions will integrate:

automated lockdown recovery

external watchdog systems

predictive drift modeling

14. Internal Philosophy
The Stability & Recovery Engine is built on:

resilience

predictability

emotional safety

cognitive stability

modular independence

It is not designed to “fix” ARUHAN — it is designed to prevent ARUHAN from breaking.

15. Notes for Trusted Engineers
Stability is the backbone of long‑term operation.

All modifications must be tested under stress conditions.

Never disable recovery in production mode.

Lockdown behavior will expand in Phase 3.
